--TEST--
`Core\Secret::reveal` and `::revealBytes` change nothing about the value — the qualifier is a compile-time judgement with no run-time representation, at both bases and at the empty edge — ADR 0033 §§ 1, 3
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `secret` lives in `nvs_types::ty::Ty` and not in `nvs_ir::ty::Ty`, so there
// is nothing at run time for a reveal to strip: the helper hands back the very
// value it was given. That is worth a case rather than an assumption, because a
// member that quietly normalized its operand — trimmed it, re-encoded it — would
// corrupt exactly the credentials this surface exists to hand over intact.
secret string $empty = "";
secret string $spaced = "  two  ";
secret bytes $noBytes = "" as bytes;
secret bytes $key = "raw-key" as bytes;

string $revealedEmpty = Core\Secret::reveal($empty, "the empty edge is still a value");
string $revealedSpaced = Core\Secret::reveal($spaced, "the padding is part of the credential");
bytes $revealedNone = Core\Secret::revealBytes($noBytes, "the empty edge, one base over");
bytes $revealedKey = Core\Secret::revealBytes($key, "the signer takes the key as bytes");

// Lengths first: a member that trimmed, padded or re-encoded shows up here
// whatever the bracketed form below prints.
echo Core\Str::length($revealedEmpty), " ", Core\Str::length($revealedSpaced), "\n";
echo Core\Bytes::length($revealedNone), " ", Core\Bytes::length($revealedKey), "\n";

// And the bytes themselves, bracketed so the surrounding whitespace is visible
// in the expectation rather than swallowed by it.
echo "[", $revealedEmpty, "][", $revealedSpaced, "]\n";
--EXPECT--
0 7
0 7
[][  two  ]
